WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.350
Film Courage: We had a comment that came in
which said [in response to a video interview]

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:07.080
“That’s easy for this person to say because
this person is a professional screenwriter

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.840
and it’s their job to write all day.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:13.380
It makes sense to say it’s your job (kind
of like get over it, just do it).

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.150
But most people that watch the Film Courage
[Youtube] channel [Which is very nice that

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:22.840
they think this] are not professional screenwriters
they actually have other jobs that have to

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.170
pay the bills.

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:28.720
Screenwriting is a hobby for them at best
while they make the two worlds work and that

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.369
I get.

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:33.020
So no…you don’t have to write everyday
to be a writer.”

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.480
Mark Sanderson: I don’t write every day.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.579
I do when I am on assignment and then when
I’m not, I’m kind of exhausted.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:44.790
And the last year has been crazy I mean with
like 6 movies so it’s like almost every

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.290
3 months turnaround.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:51.520
But it’s fantastic because I was constantly
writing but it’s draining.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.560
It’s almost like I want to go do something
else and stop.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.430
And they say “Well, when you stop the assignment,
you want to go work on your spec.”

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:03.370
Like not right now because I don’t have
the time you know?

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:07.189
But you have to manage your time, you really
do and there is no excuse.

00:01:07.189 --> 00:01:10.899
I’ve had to do it when I was in college
in film school.

00:01:10.899 --> 00:01:13.970
I was working 4 to 5 nights a week in a restaurant.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:18.329
But I had friends who went to film school
or any college and they could just go to school.

00:01:18.329 --> 00:01:21.679
They had the luxury of living at home, their
parents paid for everything.

00:01:21.679 --> 00:01:24.450
I had to pay my bills.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:32.549
But I think that trained me into really sectioning
my time.

00:01:32.549 --> 00:01:36.750
Yeah you can go to parties and all this stuff
and I was not only making movies in film school

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:42.849
but I was also after day class I had to go
down (I was working in Westwood here) and

00:01:42.849 --> 00:01:46.380
work a 5-hour shift and then come home.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.170
So it really trained me for that.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:51.670
And there’s a million excuses why we don’t
want to write.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:56.439
I mean truthfully when it’s difficult, even
more “Oh, you know?”

00:01:56.439 --> 00:02:03.130
Like I said, I never have the house as clean
as when I am procrastinating.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:10.009
But the movie I just turned in on assignment
was my 35th screenplay.

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:15.269
It’s still learning, I still respect the
craft and bow down to it because I learn something

00:02:15.269 --> 00:02:16.340
every time.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:19.849
Every project is different, every producer
or company you work with, it’s a while different

00:02:19.849 --> 00:02:21.689
scenario each time.

00:02:21.689 --> 00:02:26.180
Yes, you know how it works a lot better.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:33.379
I look at old scripts now and like my 4th
spec (my 5th one sold), my 4th one made some

00:02:33.379 --> 00:02:34.379
noise.

00:02:34.379 --> 00:02:39.069
We met with the A-list actors about it (this
was a million years ago) but I looked back

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:41.130
at that script and I was like “Arrgghh!

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:46.599
If we’d only, if I could only go back and
rewrite that with what I know now but you

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:51.190
only have the knowledge that you have this
exact moment in time and you can’t live

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:56.959
with regrets, so study, get better, write
more screenplays.

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:03.299
Yes writers write, but I don’t think you
should feel guilty if you’re not writing

00:03:03.299 --> 00:03:08.599
every day but don’t let it go two days because
two leads to four, four leads to a week, this

00:03:08.599 --> 00:03:12.989
weekend we’ve got something going on, you
have to schedule it and I always say to aspiring

00:03:12.989 --> 00:03:16.920
screenwriters, treat it like your job because
it will be.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.440
You don’t have the luxury of when you write
your specs to go “Uh, I’m going to go

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:21.749
off today.”

00:03:21.749 --> 00:03:24.159
Work on it for 5 minutes, 10 minutes, whatever
it is.

00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:30.230
But that constant is what is going to happen
when you get the assignment jobs and you don’t

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:34.870
want to be shocked when suddenly you get this
opportunity, you’re hired to write a movie

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:40.749
and you can’t physically sit there for 8
hours a day or you can’t write it in 30

00:03:40.749 --> 00:03:44.751
days or you can’t write it in 8 weeks or
however much time you’ve agreed to write

00:03:44.751 --> 00:03:45.751
the screenplay.

00:03:45.751 --> 00:03:47.959
You will be shocked because you’re not training.

00:03:47.959 --> 00:03:54.889
The same thing as an athlete or you wouldn’t
go run a marathon without training for it,

00:03:54.889 --> 00:03:57.519
same thing in my opinion, in my experience.

00:03:57.519 --> 00:04:03.239
And so if you’r going to spend the time
writing your specs, train yourself now for

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:07.700
the (hopefully) for the time that you are
going to be hired to write screenplays and

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:08.950
then you’ll be much better.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:16.150
But again…yes…coming from a thing that
this is all I do for a living but that’s

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:17.829
where you want to be.

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:24.680
And like I say, I better damn well be doing
it 10 hours a day if that’s all I do.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:28.319
Film Courage: It seems like maybe some jobs
are more draining than others.

00:04:28.319 --> 00:04:31.770
And I know it’s easy for me to just say
“Oh, well just find a job that’s not draining!”

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:33.750
And I know that’s easier said than done.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:40.630
But I think there are jobs where you can have
sort of a side other job or slash hobby and

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:44.639
there are jobs where it just takes you out
of it, it’s so demanding.

00:04:44.639 --> 00:04:48.759
Mark Sanderson: And the thing about that is
it’s going to take you that much longer

00:04:48.759 --> 00:04:52.199
if you can only write on the weekends, how
long do you think your screenplay is going

00:04:52.199 --> 00:04:53.199
to take.

00:04:53.199 --> 00:04:54.310
It’s going to take 6 months.

00:04:54.310 --> 00:05:00.259
You know, I mean that’s where the time creeps
in and there’s so many other distractions

00:05:00.259 --> 00:05:06.060
and interruptions that come in that 6 months
that are dangerous, that will derail you so

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.080
quickly and go “hmmm.”

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:16.130
That’s where you have to…I would dig deep
and say “This is what I want to do and there’s

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:17.130
nothing…”

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:20.780
That’s where the passion and the tenacity
come from that really drives you through all

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:24.330
those other, through the field.

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:25.790
You are going to step on that - Kaboom!

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:27.860
And that sets you back 3 months.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:30.130
It’s really difficult.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:31.200
And yes, you are right.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.720
When I worked at the law firm for 2 years,
it was draining.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:36.530
And I would come home and have to write again.

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:43.770
But it was even worse because I had a movie
go into production while I was at the job

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.170
and again it wasn’t enough money to quit.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:50.990
And so I had to excuse myself and say “I’m
going to go to the set for a few days this

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:57.590
week because I was an as needed assistant
type position and that was again (even years

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:00.470
later) a freaky thing going “But why do
I have to work at the thing?

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:01.470
I’ve got a movie.”

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:04.370
And then you look at the thing and you go
okay I get it.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:10.460
And then I stayed on it for another year at
the firm and do the same thing.

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:16.159
Come home, make dinner, write, you know, blah,
blah, blah and then I got a call from the

00:06:16.159 --> 00:06:19.960
production company that I worked with (the
new president of production) and they said

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:20.960
“Hey!

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:22.819
How come we haven’t worked with you since
the last movie?”

00:06:22.819 --> 00:06:23.819
I said “I don’t know?”

00:06:23.819 --> 00:06:27.980
They said “We have this fast track movie
we want you to write.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:30.240
It’s a done deal.”

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:31.930
And I gave my 2-week notice.

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:38.750
I was like this is my kick in my pants that
I’m back in without fear not like “Oh,

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:39.750
well…”

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.400
And the money was good at the firm.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:48.300
But I was seeing myself be derailed and I
had a relationship at the time (my girlfriend)

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:53.330
and I saw just everything was being derailed
and I thought well I could just get into this

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:57.199
and you know, another year go by and…

00:06:57.199 --> 00:07:00.889
So when that call came in from the company
I was like “I gotta go.”

00:07:00.889 --> 00:07:02.150
And they were shocked.

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:04.340
They were like “Well…well…I guess this
is what you do?”

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:05.340
And I said “Yeah.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:06.340
This is what I do.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:07.789
I’m a writer.

00:07:07.789 --> 00:07:10.879
I have to go do writing and it’s a job.”

00:07:10.879 --> 00:07:13.710
And they said “You are always welcome to
come back.”

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:15.310
And I said “Exactly.”

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:19.729
Like when I finally quit the restaurant business
when I got my first job, it took me 6 years

00:07:19.729 --> 00:07:24.690
out of film school before I landed my first
professional writing job and I thought when

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:27.889
they handed out the diplomas they were going
to give me a 2 picture deal.

00:07:27.889 --> 00:07:29.310
“Oh thank you very much.

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:30.650
Oh look at this?”

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.189
6 years!

00:07:32.189 --> 00:07:35.752
And that’s not for lack of writing and specs
and all of that stuff.

00:07:35.752 --> 00:07:41.240
I finally landed a job so I gave the notice
at the restaurant and they were like “Huh?”

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:45.759
They didn’t quite understand and they were
like “So how long is this job for?”

00:07:45.759 --> 00:07:47.289
And I go “No!

00:07:47.289 --> 00:07:48.289
I’m out.

00:07:48.289 --> 00:07:49.289
O-U-T.

00:07:49.289 --> 00:07:50.289
Not coming back.”

00:07:50.289 --> 00:07:53.259
I said “I appreciate…there’s never again
will I step foot…”

00:07:53.259 --> 00:07:54.290
And I didn’t.

00:07:54.290 --> 00:08:00.470
I mean I did catering but that was sort of
less of a, you’re in different places.

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:02.960
But I was never going to work…I worked 10
years in a restaurant.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:06.199
I did many odd jobs in my life, I say in the
book.

00:08:06.199 --> 00:08:12.820
Many different jobs but it was all about facilitating
me being able to stick with my dream, whatever

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:17.990
the job was, as long as it allowed me to continue,
I didn’t care.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:20.099
It wasn’t embarrassing or you know.

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:21.940
It was like you have to do what you have to
do.

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:25.770
Film Courage: It reminds me of the movie I
just watched IT’S A WONDERFUL LIFE.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:30.129
So Jimmy Stewart he thinks I’m going to
go off and see the world and then go be an

00:08:30.129 --> 00:08:37.590
architect and then gets sucked back in 2 years
or 4 years at the bank and it keeps…life

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:38.590
changed for him.

00:08:38.590 --> 00:08:39.590
Mark Sanderson: It’s hard.

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:41.890
It really is hard.

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:46.950
But as I say it’s hard, I am living proof
that it does happen.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:51.190
And there’s no guarantees but I always say
the only guarantee in screenwriting is that

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:57.910
if you should stop writing, you’re definitely
guaranteed never to have any chance at success.

00:08:57.910 --> 00:09:01.100
So as long as you’re in the game, you have
a shot.

